div.p:
‘But has he promised thus to save us from sin while we are in the body?’ Undoubtedly he has; for a promise is implied in every commandment of God;
See above, II.1, 2; also No. 25, ‘Sermon on the Mount, V’, II.2 and n. consequently in that, ‘Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thy heart, and with all thy soul, and with all thy mind.’ For this and every other commandment is given, not to the dead, but to the living. It is expressed in the words above recited, that we should walk ‘in holiness before him all the days of our life’.
Luke 1:75.

12. But a still more plausible objection remains, taken from experience;
                    which is, that there are no living witnesses of this salvation from sin. In
                    answer to this I allow,

happy in their mistake. Else, be their opinion right or wrong, your temper is undeniably sinful. Bear then with us, as we do with you; and see whether the Lord will not deliver us;
Cf. Wesley’s Preface (1746), §9. whether he is not able, yea, and willing ‘to save them to the uttermost that come unto God through him’.
Heb. 7:25.
